__d(function(e,a,t,i,s,o,n){s.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:16,chapter_title:"Garbage In, Garbage Out",quiz_type:"MCQ only",mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is a biodegradable waste item?",options:["A) Plastic bag","B) Glass bottle","C) Vegetable peel","D) Aluminium can"],correct_answer:"C) Vegetable peel",explanation:"Vegetable peels are biodegradable \u2014 they are organic matter that microorganisms (bacteria, fungi) can decompose into simpler substances within days to weeks. Plastic bags, glass bottles, and aluminium cans are all non-biodegradable \u2014 they persist in the environment for decades to millions of years without breaking down."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is a non-biodegradable waste item?",options:["A) Paper","B) Leaves","C) Cooked food","D) Plastic wrapper"],correct_answer:"D) Plastic wrapper",explanation:"Plastic wrappers are non-biodegradable \u2014 they are made from petroleum-derived polymers that microorganisms cannot break down; they persist for 100\u20131000 years. Paper is biodegradable (made from wood fibres). Leaves and cooked food are biodegradable organic matter. Students often mistake paper for non-biodegradable \u2014 it is not."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is a landfill?",options:["A) A place where composting is done","B) A large site where garbage is deposited and buried","C) A factory where waste is recycled into new products","D) A water treatment plant"],correct_answer:"B) A large site where garbage is deposited and buried",explanation:"A landfill (garbage dump) is a large area of land where municipal solid waste is deposited, compacted, and buried. It is the most common but least sustainable method of waste disposal. Problems include leachate (toxic liquid contaminating groundwater), methane gas production, disease vectors, and rapid capacity limits."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is composting?",options:["A) Burning garbage to produce electricity","B) Converting biodegradable organic waste into manure (compost) through microbial decomposition","C) Separating plastic from other garbage","D) Burying all types of waste underground"],correct_answer:"B) Converting biodegradable organic waste into manure (compost) through microbial decomposition",explanation:"Composting is the process by which microorganisms (bacteria, fungi) break down biodegradable organic waste (vegetable peels, food scraps, leaves) into compost \u2014 a rich, dark, crumbly manure that improves soil fertility. It takes 4\u20138 weeks and can be done in a pit or bin. It diverts biodegradable waste from landfills and returns nutrients to the soil."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is vermicomposting?",options:["A) Composting using high-temperature furnaces","B) Composting using earthworms to decompose organic waste","C) Composting only vegetable and fruit waste","D) A chemical process to decompose plastic"],correct_answer:"B) Composting using earthworms to decompose organic waste",explanation:"Vermicomposting uses earthworms (usually the Red Wiggler species) to break down organic waste. Earthworms eat the waste and their castings (excretions) form vermicompost \u2014 a nutrient-rich manure superior to regular compost. It is faster (2\u20134 weeks) and produces a richer product than conventional composting. It can be set up in a small bin at home or school."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does the first R in the 3Rs stand for, and why is it the most important?",options:["A) Recycle \u2014 because it converts waste into new materials","B) Reuse \u2014 because old items can be used again","C) Reduce \u2014 because preventing waste is better than managing it after it is created","D) Recover \u2014 because energy can be recovered from waste"],correct_answer:"C) Reduce \u2014 because preventing waste is better than managing it after it is created",explanation:"Reduce is the first and most important R. It means generating less waste in the first place by using fewer resources, avoiding unnecessary packaging, and choosing reusable over single-use items. Reducing waste avoids all the subsequent costs and impacts of collection, processing, and disposal. It is always better to not create waste than to manage it afterward."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"How many trees are saved when 1 tonne of paper is recycled instead of made from virgin wood?",options:["A) 5 trees","B) 10 trees","C) 17 trees","D) 50 trees"],correct_answer:"C) 17 trees",explanation:"Recycling 1 tonne of paper saves approximately 17 trees from being cut down for wood pulp. It also saves about 26,000 litres of water and significant energy. Paper recycling is one of the most impactful individual and institutional actions to reduce environmental harm. Every newspaper, notebook, or cardboard box given to a recycler potentially saves trees."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Why is burning garbage harmful?",options:["A) It creates too much ash that is difficult to clean up","B) It releases toxic gases including dioxins, carbon monoxide, and particulate matter causing air pollution and health problems","C) It uses too much electricity","D) It produces water that contaminates rivers"],correct_answer:"B) It releases toxic gases including dioxins, carbon monoxide, and particulate matter causing air pollution and health problems",explanation:"Burning garbage releases highly toxic compounds: dioxins and furans (carcinogenic), carbon monoxide (poisonous gas), particulate matter (lung irritants), carbon dioxide (greenhouse gas), and other toxic fumes from burning plastics. These cause respiratory diseases, increase cancer risk, and contribute to air pollution and global warming. Open burning of garbage is illegal under India's Solid Waste Management Rules 2016."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Karnataka banned which type of plastic to reduce non-biodegradable waste?",options:["A) All types of plastic permanently","B) Single-use plastics under 40 micron thickness (2016)","C) Only plastic used in hospitals","D) Plastic used only in food packaging"],correct_answer:"B) Single-use plastics under 40 micron thickness (2016)",explanation:"Karnataka implemented a ban on single-use plastic items with thickness under 40 microns in 2016 \u2014 one of the first states in India to do so. Thin single-use plastics (carry bags, straws, cutlery) are the most pervasive form of plastic pollution as they are lightweight, easily dispersed, and rarely recycled. This was a direct application of the 'Reduce' principle at the policy level."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"In Bengaluru's BBMP two-bin system, which bin should vegetable peels be placed in?",options:["A) Blue/white bin (dry waste)","B) Red bin (hazardous)","C) Green bin (wet/biodegradable waste)","D) Yellow bin (recyclables)"],correct_answer:"C) Green bin (wet/biodegradable waste)",explanation:"Vegetable peels are biodegradable wet waste \u2014 they go in the green bin. BBMP's two-bin system: green bin = wet/biodegradable waste (food scraps, peels, garden waste) \u2192 goes to composting or biogas plants; white/blue bin = dry waste (plastic, paper, glass, metal) \u2192 goes to Dry Waste Collection Centres for recycling. Keeping these streams separate is essential for effective waste management."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A landfill produces leachate. Which of the following best describes its danger?",options:["A) Leachate is a foul-smelling gas that pollutes the air around the landfill","B) Leachate is a toxic liquid that forms when rainwater passes through garbage and contaminates groundwater","C) Leachate is methane gas produced by anaerobic decomposition of waste","D) Leachate is the name for the solid residue left after burning garbage"],correct_answer:"B) Leachate is a toxic liquid that forms when rainwater passes through garbage and contaminates groundwater",explanation:"Leachate forms when rainwater percolates through a landfill, dissolving heavy metals (lead, mercury, cadmium), toxic organic compounds, and pathogens from the garbage. This contaminated liquid seeps into the soil and groundwater below, polluting drinking water sources for nearby communities. It is one of the primary environmental hazards of landfills, especially those without proper liner systems."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following actions is the best example of 'Reuse' from the 3Rs?",options:["A) Giving old newspapers to a kabadiwala for recycling","B) Using both sides of a piece of paper before discarding it","C) Buying a smaller bottle of shampoo","D) Composting vegetable peels"],correct_answer:"B) Using both sides of a piece of paper before discarding it",explanation:"Using both sides of a paper is Reuse \u2014 the item (paper) is used again (on its blank side) before being discarded. Giving newspapers to a kabadiwala is Recycling (converting waste to raw material). Buying a smaller bottle reduces waste (Reduce). Composting is a form of recycling organic material. Reuse means extending the life of an item by using it more than once for its original or another purpose."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Vermicompost is considered superior to regular compost. Why?",options:["A) It takes longer to produce, making it more valuable","B) It is richer in nutrients (N, P, K) and beneficial microorganisms due to processing through earthworm guts","C) It can be used to decompose non-biodegradable waste","D) It contains plastic that strengthens the soil"],correct_answer:"B) It is richer in nutrients (N, P, K) and beneficial microorganisms due to processing through earthworm guts",explanation:"Earthworm guts contain powerful enzymes and microorganisms that break organic matter into extremely fine particles and convert nutrients into highly plant-available forms. The result \u2014 vermicompost (worm castings) \u2014 has much higher concentrations of nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium, and beneficial microbes than regular compost. It is also produced faster (2\u20134 weeks vs. 4\u20138 weeks for regular composting)."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Bengaluru generated 5000\u20136000 tonnes of garbage per day but its landfills overflowed. What forced change in 2012?",options:["A) A new law banning all plastic in Bengaluru","B) Villagers near the Mandur landfill blocking garbage trucks due to pollution, forcing BBMP to implement source segregation","C) A flood that washed away the landfill","D) The central government ordering all landfills closed"],correct_answer:"B) Villagers near the Mandur landfill blocking garbage trucks due to pollution, forcing BBMP to implement source segregation",explanation:"In 2012, communities near Mandur (30 km from Bengaluru) blockaded garbage trucks because the overflowing landfill was contaminating their water and air. Garbage piled up across Bengaluru. This crisis forced BBMP to implement mandatory two-bin source segregation (wet/dry), set up decentralised composting, and establish Dry Waste Collection Centres \u2014 reforms that fundamentally changed how Bengaluru manages its waste."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following correctly explains why plastic waste is more serious than paper waste in a landfill?",options:["A) Plastic is heavier than paper, taking up more landfill space","B) Plastic is non-biodegradable and persists for centuries, while paper biodegrades within weeks to months","C) Plastic generates more methane than paper during decomposition","D) Plastic is more expensive than paper and is wasted economically"],correct_answer:"B) Plastic is non-biodegradable and persists for centuries, while paper biodegrades within weeks to months",explanation:"Paper is biodegradable \u2014 microorganisms decompose it within weeks to months. Plastic is non-biodegradable \u2014 most common plastics take 100\u20131000 years to break down. In a landfill, paper eventually becomes part of the soil, while plastic simply accumulates. Additionally, plastic slowly breaks into microplastics that contaminate soil and groundwater. This is why plastic waste is far more persistent and damaging than paper waste."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"In a school with 500 students, each student uses 2 plastic disposable cups per day for water. What would be the BEST approach to reduce this plastic waste?",options:["A) Recycle all the cups at the end of each day","B) Compost the cups as biodegradable waste","C) Each student brings and uses their own reusable steel or glass water bottle \u2014 eliminating disposable cup use entirely","D) Use thicker plastic cups that last longer before disposal"],correct_answer:"C) Each student brings and uses their own reusable steel or glass water bottle \u2014 eliminating disposable cup use entirely",explanation:"This question tests understanding of the 3Rs hierarchy. Recycling (Option A) still requires energy and produces some waste. Composting (Option B) is wrong \u2014 plastic cups are non-biodegradable and cannot be composted. Thicker cups (Option D) still create plastic waste. The BEST solution is Reduce + Reuse: each student using their own reusable bottle eliminates 1000 disposable cups per day \u2014 zero waste generated, zero to manage."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What type of waste must NOT be added to a regular compost pit?",options:["A) Vegetable peels and fruit waste","B) Dry leaves and garden waste","C) Meat, oily food, and dairy products","D) Paper scraps and cardboard"],correct_answer:"C) Meat, oily food, and dairy products",explanation:"Meat, oily food, dairy products, and fish should NOT be added to a regular compost pit. These items: (1) Attract pests \u2014 rats, flies, stray animals. (2) Create foul odours due to the type of bacteria that decomposes proteins and fats (different from those in standard composting). (3) Can harbour pathogens. Suitable compost inputs: vegetable and fruit peels, dry leaves, garden waste, plain cooked grains (without gravy), paper. Egg shells are acceptable \u2014 they provide calcium."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A student argues that recycling is the most important R because it converts waste into new materials. Is this correct?",options:["A) Yes \u2014 recycling is always the best way to manage waste","B) No \u2014 Reduce is more important because it prevents waste from being created, eliminating the need to manage it","C) Yes \u2014 recycling saves more energy than reducing or reusing","D) No \u2014 Reuse is more important than Reduce because reusing saves money"],correct_answer:"B) No \u2014 Reduce is more important because it prevents waste from being created, eliminating the need to manage it",explanation:"The 3Rs have a priority order: Reduce > Reuse > Recycle. Recycling is the third and least preferred option. While better than landfilling, recycling still requires energy, water, and transport to process. Reducing waste prevents all these downstream costs and impacts. Not generating waste is always better than any method of managing it afterward. The student is correct that recycling is valuable \u2014 but it should be the last resort, not the primary strategy."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"A village has rich organic waste (crop residue, kitchen waste) but poor, degraded farmland. Which of the following is the MOST sustainable solution that addresses both problems simultaneously?",options:["A) Build a landfill to dispose of all organic waste","B) Burn crop residue to clear the fields faster before the next planting","C) Set up vermicomposting units using organic waste to produce vermicompost for the degraded farmland","D) Send all organic waste to a city recycling plant far away"],correct_answer:"C) Set up vermicomposting units using organic waste to produce vermicompost for the degraded farmland",explanation:"This is the ideal circular solution: (1) Organic waste (a problem) is converted into vermicompost by earthworms. (2) Vermicompost (a solution) is applied to degraded farmland, restoring its fertility and structure. (3) The farm then produces more crops, which generates more organic waste \u2014 which feeds the next cycle. This is a closed-loop system with no landfill, no burning, no transport cost. Burning crop residue (Option B) destroys nutrients, pollutes air, and degrades soil \u2014 the worst option."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"Analyse: Why does urbanisation \u2014 replacing open land with buildings and roads \u2014 worsen the garbage crisis?",options:["A) Urban people are lazier than rural people and generate more waste","B) Urbanisation increases population density and consumption, generating more waste per unit area; impermeable surfaces prevent organic matter from decomposing naturally; and traditional waste management systems (composting, land disposal) are no longer feasible","C) Urban buildings prevent sunlight from reaching garbage, stopping natural decomposition","D) Urban roads make it harder for garbage trucks to collect waste efficiently"],correct_answer:"B) Urbanisation increases population density and consumption, generating more waste per unit area; impermeable surfaces prevent organic matter from decomposing naturally; and traditional waste management systems (composting, land disposal) are no longer feasible",explanation:"Urbanisation worsens waste problems through multiple mechanisms: (1) Higher consumption per person = more packaging waste, food waste, and e-waste. (2) Concrete and asphalt surfaces prevent organic matter from decomposing naturally in soil. (3) Urban density means there is no space for on-site composting or waste disposal. (4) The 'throwaway economy' of cities creates far more single-use packaging. (5) Traditional village-level solutions (farm pits, local composting) cannot scale to cities of millions. This is why cities like Bengaluru require systematic, engineered waste management solutions."}]}},2726,[]);